            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many protein powders contain trace amounts of lead, because it is present in soil and therefore in crops. Independent testing has generally found more in plant-based and chocolate-flavored products than in whey and vanilla. Amounts vary widely between brands and batches, so the most reliable signal is a product that publishes batch results for heavy metals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is plant protein less safe than whey?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not inherently, but plant proteins have tended to test higher for lead, cadmium and arsenic because the crops absorb metals from soil. Whey carries milk allergy and lactose concerns that plant protein avoids. A well-tested plant powder can be a better choice than an untested whey, so testing transparency matters more than the protein source alone."
          }
        },
