        <div id="prop-65" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What a Prop 65 Warning Does and Does Not Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            California's Proposition 65, approved by voters in 1986, requires businesses to warn consumers about exposures to chemicals the state lists as causing cancer, birth defects or other reproductive harm. Lead is on the list, as are cadmium and inorganic arsenic. If a product sold in California may expose someone to more than the state's safe harbor level, it needs a warning. That is why many protein powders carry one, often on the label or product page for every buyer, not only Californians.
          </p>

          <p>
            The thresholds are deliberately very conservative. For lead, the reproductive-harm threshold is 0.5 micrograms per day, set at one-thousandth of the level at which no effect was observed in the underlying studies. Because many foods contain trace lead from soil, a single serving of an ordinary plant food can cross that line. A Prop 65 warning means exposure may exceed that cautious threshold. It is not a finding that the product is dangerous at normal use.
          </p>

          <p>
            The warning also cuts the other way: its absence does not prove a product is clean. Some companies add warnings defensively to avoid lawsuits whether or not their product needs one, while others with similar contamination levels simply have not been challenged. On its own, the label tells you surprisingly little about how one brand compares with another.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a Prop 65 warning should prompt is a question, not panic. Look for a brand that publishes heavy metal results by batch or holds a certification that includes contaminant testing, and give the warning more weight if you are pregnant, planning a pregnancy, or buying for a teenager. For an adult using one scoop a day, a warning on a well-tested product is less concerning than an untested product with no warning and no data at all.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READING THE WARNING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A Prop 65 warning is a flag, not a verdict</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It says exposure may exceed one of the most cautious limits anywhere. It does not tell you how much lead is in a scoop, and a missing warning proves nothing. Published batch test results tell you far more.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="labels-banned-substances" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Label Accuracy, Amino Spiking and Banned Substances
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein content is often estimated by measuring nitrogen, because protein is the main nitrogen-containing nutrient in food. That created a loophole. Some brands added cheap, nitrogen-rich amino acids such as glycine or taurine, or compounds like creatine, which pushed the measured protein figure up without adding the complete protein buyers were paying for. The practice, known as amino spiking, prompted a wave of class-action lawsuits in the mid-2010s, and many brands now advertise that they do not do it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spiking is harder to hide than it used to be, but it is still worth checking. Look at the ingredient list for free amino acids such as glycine, taurine, glutamine or creatine listed separately from the protein source. A published amino acid profile is a good sign; a proprietary blend that hides amounts is not. Also compare protein per scoop with scoop size. A 30-gram scoop of whey isolate claiming about 25 grams of protein is plausible. A 30-gram scoop of a heavily flavored blend claiming 27 grams deserves skepticism.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more serious contamination risk is undeclared drugs. Supplements have repeatedly been found to contain substances not on the label, including anabolic steroids, prohormones and stimulants, sometimes through deliberate adulteration and sometimes through cross-contamination on shared equipment. A study commissioned by the International Olympic Committee in the early 2000s found that roughly 15 percent of the nonhormonal supplements it analyzed contained undeclared prohormones. Protein powders are lower risk than muscle-building or weight-loss products, but they are often made in the same kinds of facilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tested athletes this is a career issue, not just a health one. Under the World Anti-Doping Agency code, athletes are strictly liable for anything found in their system, and a contaminated supplement is generally not a defense. That is why many college and professional organizations tell athletes to use only products carrying NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification. Both programs screen for a long list of banned substances. Informed Sport tests every batch before release, while NSF Certified for Sport combines product testing, label claim verification and facility audits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification only helps if you confirm it. Logos can be copied, certifications lapse, and a brand may certify one product or flavor but not another. Search the certifier's own database, either the NSF Certified for Sport website and app or the Informed Sport search tool, by brand and exact product name, and check that the flavor and size you are buying are listed. Athletes should also note the lot number, since Informed Sport lets you check whether a specific batch was tested. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder rankings</a> flag certifications, but the certifier's database is the final word because listings change.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Program</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it covers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best fit</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned substance screening, label claim checks, contaminant screening and facility audits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tested athletes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Informed Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned substance testing of every batch before release</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tested athletes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Informed Choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned substance testing of regular samples rather than every batch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recreational lifters</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GMP registration alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Manufacturing practices at the facility, not testing of the finished product</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A baseline quality signal only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1994</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DSHEA: no FDA approval needed before sale</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.5 mcg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Prop 65 daily lead threshold for reproductive harm</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~15%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Supplements with undeclared prohormones in an IOC-commissioned study</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">400 mg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily caffeine the FDA says is generally safe for healthy adults</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-be-careful" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Kidney and Liver Myths, and Who Should Genuinely Be Careful
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most persistent myth is that protein powder damages healthy kidneys. Higher protein intakes do raise the kidneys' filtration rate, but that is a normal adaptation, much as exercise raises heart rate. Studies in healthy adults eating well above the RDA, including the 1.6 to 2.2 g/kg range common among lifters, have not shown kidney damage. The same applies to the liver: there is no good evidence that protein itself harms a healthy liver.
          </p>

          <p>
            Existing kidney disease is a different story. People with chronic kidney disease are often advised to limit protein, because damaged kidneys have to work harder to clear the waste products of protein metabolism, and excess protein may speed decline. The right target depends on the stage of disease, diabetes status and whether the person is on dialysis, since people on dialysis often need more protein rather than less. If you have CKD, reduced kidney function on a blood test, or a history of kidney stones, talk to your nephrologist or a registered dietitian before adding a powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liver disease, gout and some metabolic conditions also call for individual advice rather than a general rule. Advanced liver disease can change how the body handles protein, and some powders include herbal extracts, such as green tea extract, that have been linked to rare cases of liver injury, usually at high doses. In these situations the protein is rarely the main problem; the extra ingredients and the underlying condition are what need a doctor's eye.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pregnancy and breastfeeding</h3>

          <p>
            Protein needs rise during pregnancy, with a common recommendation of about 1.1 g/kg per day in the second half, which many people can reach from food. If a powder helps, the priorities shift toward contaminants and additives. Choose a product with published heavy metal testing, lean toward whey or a well-tested blend over unverified plant powders, avoid herbal and stimulant extras, and check for added vitamin A or other fortification that could stack with a prenatal vitamin. Run the specific product past your obstetric provider or a registered dietitian, and do the same while breastfeeding.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Medications, children and teens</h3>

          <p>
            A few interactions are worth knowing. Protein competes with levodopa for absorption, so people taking it for Parkinson's disease are often told to time protein carefully. Calcium- or mineral-fortified shakes can reduce absorption of certain antibiotics, such as tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones, and of thyroid medication like levothyroxine when taken together. Children and most teens can meet their needs from food and rarely need protein powder at all. If you take regular medication, ask a pharmacist about timing; it takes two minutes and costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergies-digestion-stimulants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Allergies, Digestive Side Effects and Caffeinated Blends
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milk is one of the most common food allergens, and whey and casein are both milk proteins. A true milk allergy is an immune reaction, not a digestive inconvenience, and even whey isolate, which has most of the lactose removed, still contains the proteins that trigger it. Soy is another major allergen, found in soy isolates and many blends. Pea protein is increasingly common, and because peas are legumes, some people with a peanut allergy react to it. Read the full ingredient list and allergen statement, including warnings about shared equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose intolerance is a separate and far more common issue. Whey concentrate contains some lactose; whey isolate is filtered further and usually contains less than a gram per serving, which many lactose-intolerant people tolerate well. If concentrate gives you bloating or cramps, an isolate is the logical next step, and our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide covers the options. If dairy in any form disagrees with you, our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> picks cover the alternatives, with the heavy metal caveats above in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many digestive complaints blamed on protein actually come from what is added to it. Sugar alcohols such as sorbitol, maltitol and xylitol, and to a lesser degree erythritol, can cause gas and loose stools in larger amounts. Added fibers like inulin or chicory root are frequent culprits, as are some thickening gums. If a powder disagrees with you, check for these before giving up on protein powder entirely. Xylitol is also highly toxic to dogs, so keep tubs and shaker bottles out of their reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            A newer category blends protein with pre-workout ingredients: caffeine, sometimes other stimulants, plus extras like beta-alanine or herbal extracts. One serving can contain as much caffeine as a strong coffee, or more. The FDA says up to about 400 mg of caffeine a day is generally not associated with dangerous effects in healthy adults, but that ceiling is easy to reach if you also drink coffee or take a separate pre-workout. Pregnant people are commonly advised to stay under about 200 mg a day, and people with heart rhythm problems, high blood pressure or anxiety may need to stay lower still.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to read caffeine per serving on every product you use, add it up across the day, and avoid stacking two caffeinated shakes. Avoid caffeinated protein late in the day if sleep matters to your training, which it does. If a blend lists a proprietary energy complex without amounts, you cannot know what you are taking, and that alone is a reason to choose something else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safer-powder-checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Practical Checklist for a Safer Powder, and How Much Is Too Much
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the protection you can give yourself comes from a short checklist. None of these steps requires lab access, and together they screen out most of the problems described above. If a product fails two or more of them, there is almost always a better-documented alternative at a similar cost per serving.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Check</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to look for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party certification</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice, confirmed in the certifier's database</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Independent screening for banned substances, plus contaminants for some programs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy metal results</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Batch results published or provided on request</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The only direct evidence of lead, cadmium and arsenic levels</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Transparent label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full amino acid profile; no proprietary blends hiding amounts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Guards against amino spiking and hidden stimulants</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Short ingredient list</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Protein source, flavoring, sweetener and few extras</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fewer routes for contaminants, allergens and digestive triggers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flavor choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vanilla or unflavored for daily plant protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chocolate tends to carry more cadmium and lead</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Allergen statement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk, soy, egg and shared-equipment warnings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Allergies are the most immediate risk for some buyers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seller</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand site or authorized retailer, intact seal, printed lot and date</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduces the risk of counterfeit or expired product</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The seller matters more than many buyers expect. Counterfeit and diverted supplements do turn up on third-party marketplace listings, and a fake tub can look identical to the real one. Buy from the brand directly or from a retailer authorized to sell it, check that the seal is intact and the lot number and expiration date are printed, and be wary of prices far below what the product normally sells for.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much is too much depends on your total protein intake from all sources, not the powder alone. The RDA is 0.8 g/kg per day, a minimum to prevent deficiency in sedentary adults. The International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg per day for people who exercise, and a 2018 meta-analysis by Morton and colleagues found that gains from resistance training plateaued around 1.6 g/kg, with an upper confidence limit near 2.2 g/kg. Our guide on <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> walks through the numbers by goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people that works out to one or two scoops a day alongside real meals, not in place of them. Beyond about 2.2 g/kg there is little evidence of added benefit, and every extra scoop adds a little more exposure to whatever trace contaminants the product carries, along with calories and sweeteners that crowd out other foods. If you are drinking three or four shakes a day, the better question is not whether protein powder is safe but whether you are eating enough whole food.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, know when a general guide stops being enough. If you have kidney or liver disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take regular medication, have a known food allergy, or are buying for a teenager, take the label to a doctor, pharmacist or registered dietitian before you start. A short conversation with someone who knows your history is worth more than any online ranking, including ours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a logo instead of the database</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certification logos are easy to copy, and certifications lapse. A brand may certify one flavor and not another. Before buying, and especially if you are a tested athlete, search the exact product, flavor and size in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database. If it is not listed there, treat it as uncertified.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming organic or plant-based means cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Organic rules govern farming inputs, not the lead and cadmium already in the soil. Plant proteins, organic or not, have generally tested higher for heavy metals than whey in independent reports. Plant powders can still be a good choice, but pick ones with published batch testing and favor vanilla or unflavored over chocolate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Prop 65 warning as all or nothing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some buyers throw out any tub with a Prop 65 warning, while others assume a missing warning means the product is clean. Neither holds up. The warning reflects an extremely cautious threshold and is sometimes added defensively. Actual batch test results tell you far more than whether a warning appears on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking caffeinated shakes with coffee and pre-workout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein blends sold for energy or as pre-workouts can contain as much caffeine as a strong coffee. Add a morning cup and a separate pre-workout, and it is easy to pass 400 mg in a day. Check the caffeine per serving on every product you use, and count it all together across the day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the conversation with your doctor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication such as levodopa, thyroid hormone or certain antibiotics, or are buying for a teenager, a quick talk with a doctor, pharmacist or registered dietitian is worth more than any online ranking, this one included. Bring the label so they can see every ingredient, not just the protein.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder safe to take every day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most healthy adults, yes, in normal amounts from a reputable, third-party tested brand. One or two scoops a day as part of a balanced diet is typical. Daily use does make contaminant levels matter more, so favor products that publish heavy metal testing, and talk to a doctor first if you have kidney disease or another chronic condition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does protein powder contain lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many protein powders contain trace amounts of lead, because it is present in soil and therefore in crops. Independent testing has generally found more in plant-based and chocolate-flavored products than in whey and vanilla. Amounts vary widely between brands and batches, so the most reliable signal is a product that publishes batch results for heavy metals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is plant protein less safe than whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not inherently, but plant proteins have tended to test higher for lead, cadmium and arsenic because the crops absorb metals from soil. Whey carries milk allergy and lactose concerns that plant protein avoids. A well-tested plant powder can be a better choice than an untested whey, so testing transparency matters more than the protein source alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein powder damage your kidneys?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In healthy people, research has not shown kidney damage from higher protein intakes, including the 1.6 to 2.2 g/kg range common among lifters. People with chronic kidney disease are a different case and are often advised to limit protein. If you have reduced kidney function or a history of kidney stones, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder safe during pregnancy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, but it deserves extra care. Protein needs rise during pregnancy, yet many people can meet them from food. If you use a powder, choose one with published heavy metal testing and no herbal extracts, stimulants or heavy fortification that could stack with a prenatal vitamin, and confirm the specific product with your obstetric provider or a registered dietitian.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does NSF Certified for Sport mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means NSF has screened the product for a long list of substances banned in sport, checked that the label claims match the contents, screened for contaminants and audited the manufacturing facility. It is the certification many professional and college sports organizations recognize. Always confirm the exact product and flavor in NSF's database, because listings change and logos can be misused.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does protein powder upset my stomach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual causes are lactose in whey concentrate, sugar alcohols such as sorbitol or xylitol, added fibers like inulin, or simply drinking a large serving quickly. Try a whey isolate if lactose is the issue, or a powder with a shorter ingredient list. Hives, swelling or persistent symptoms suggest an allergy or another problem and warrant a visit to your doctor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

