        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protein powder is not the hazard some headlines make it out to be, and it is not the tightly regulated product its packaging implies. No one approves it before sale, trace heavy metals are common, especially in plant and chocolate products, and label accuracy depends on the brand. For a healthy adult using one or two scoops a day from a reputable, third-party tested product, the risk is low.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Make the choice deliberately: confirm certifications in the certifier's database, prefer brands that publish heavy metal results, favor vanilla or unflavored if you use plant protein daily, and check caffeine and allergens on every label. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take regular medication, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian first. When you are ready to choose, our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings are a sensible place to start.
